Rendering the actual components against the built CSS and measuring them
in Chromium turned up three things the component tests were blind to.

The project name truncated to "SOCIAL M…" in the sidebar, where 224px were
free. The cap was max-w-[14ch] on the switcher summary, and t-meta is
uppercase with 0.16em of tracking, so 14ch there is nowhere near twelve
letters. The cap is gone; the container decides now -- the header flex
squeezes it (hence min-w-0), the sidebar column gives it the full width.

The sidebar column was an <aside>, which made it a second unnamed
complementary landmark beside the rail's named one. It is a div now; the
nav inside already carries the landmark.

The sidebar had no overflow of its own, so a column taller than the
viewport would have clipped the account block at the bottom with no way to
reach it. It scrolls only when it exceeds, which is the phase's scroll
model applied to the one place that had been left out.

Also worth recording because it nearly became a false bug report:
headless Chromium clamps the window to a 500px minimum, so a screenshot
requested at 375 renders at 500 and crops. The apparent overflow was the
measurement, not the page. Measured through an iframe instead,
scrollWidth equals clientWidth at 320, 375, 768 and 1024, and the
attention answer still fits above the fold at 375x667.

